 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Depth Accuracy:** The quality of the back-projected point cloud depends heavily on the accuracy of the input depth maps $D_i$. Noisy depth can lead to incorrect Gaussian initialization [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Occlusion:** Sparse views may result in incomplete point clouds. The self-supervised learning relies on the renderer's ability to handle occlusions via alpha blending, but missing geometry may limit feature richness [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Depth Accuracy:** The quality of the back-projected point cloud depends heavily on the accuracy of the input depth maps $D_i$ [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
@@ -144,8 +147,8 @@
 *   **Memory:** GS3 uses less than $0.25\times$ the memory cost of Ponder during pre-training [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Performance Context:** Measurements were taken at a rendered image resolution of $320 \times 240$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High efficiency in pre-training due to the use of tile-based rasterization instead of ray marching. Enables generalizable pre-training for point cloud encoders.
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the availability of RGB-D inputs for pre-training. Performance on downstream tasks depends on the quality of the pre-trained encoder [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When pre-training a point cloud encoder for downstream 3D vision tasks (segmentation, detection) with limited annotations and computational resources.
-*   **When not to use:** When only RGB inputs are available without depth, or when per-scene optimization (not pre-training) is required.
